<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Soothing Guanabana – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Soothing Guanabana</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present basic information about personal and community topics. Interactive: Exchange simple ideas in group discussions about current events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present basic information on a plant's uses (e.g., "Guanabana (soursop) is used to treat infections."). Can exchange information about medicinal plants used in Panamanian home remedies (e.g., "My grandfather Bill uses ginger tea for colds. What does your family use?"). Can ask and answer questions about plant benefits (e.g., "What is sabila (aloe vera) used for?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and grammar for active oral participation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of various medicinal plants (guanabana/soursop, ginger, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves) introduced in previous lessons. Students are asked to identify the plants and briefly share one use they remember. Teacher prompts: "What plant is this?" "What is it used for?" (e.g., "This is ginger. It's used for colds.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target structures: "used to + infinitive verb" and "should/shouldn't for advice." For example, holding up a picture of soursop: "This is guanabana. It is used to treat infections. If you have an infection, you should try guanabana." Teacher models pronunciation of compound nouns like "herbal garden" or "mint leaves", emphasizing the first syllable, and clarifies syllable division for words like "treatment" and "therapeutic" by clapping out syllables. Students repeat phrases chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using questions like:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is guanabana used to treat headaches or infections?" (Expected answer: "Infections.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend has a stomachache, should he drink a soda or ginger tea?" (Expected answer: "Ginger tea.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to "The Healing Plants of Panama," focusing on preparing students for speaking activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (e.g., 1-2 minutes) between two Panamanian teenagers discussing common family home remedies, specifically mentioning "The Soothing Guanabana" and other healing plants. The dialogue will naturally integrate the target grammar (used to, should/shouldn't, present simple for facts) and vocabulary. (e.g., "My grandma says guanabana leaves are good for infections. She boils them." "Really? My mom says you should use sábila for burns. It's very soothing.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacting orally in short exchanges to share information about common topics and give simple health advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the dialogue once for gist: "What are the two people talking about?" (Expected: home remedies/medicinal plants).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen a second time and answer True/False questions orally: "True or False: The boy's grandmother uses soursop for headaches." "True or False: The girl's mother recommends aloe vera for burns." Students justify their answers by quoting phrases from the dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking, reinforcing target grammar, vocabulary, pronunciation, and pragmatic functions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the infinitive of purpose ("used to + verb"), "should/shouldn't" for advice, and present simple for facts. Vocabulary: plant, leaf, root, remedy, ginger, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves; treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare; useful, medicinal, natural, therapeutic; for, with; traditionally, safely. Pronunciation: compound nouns (e.g., "soursop tea"), syllable division ("therapeutic"). Pragmatic: giving advice, stating purpose.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition (Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or projector, and students complete them orally using target vocabulary and grammar.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Guanabana (soursop) leaves are ________ (used to / treat) infections." (Expected: "used to treat")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"If you have a cold, you ________ (should / drink) lemongrass tea." (Expected: "should drink")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Aloe vera is a ________ (medicinal / plant)." (Expected: "medicinal plant")</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students repeat the full sentences chorally, then individually, focusing on correct pronunciation and stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Substitution Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives a prompt (e.g., "headache," "mint leaves") and a sentence frame (e.g., "For a _____, you should use _____."). Students take turns substituting the prompt into the frame, then creating a statement of purpose.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Teacher prompt: "Headache, mint leaves" -> Student 1: "For a headache, you should use mint leaves." Student 2: "Mint leaves are used to soothe headaches."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Teacher prompt: "Burns, aloe vera" -> Student 1: "For burns, you should use aloe vera." Student 2: "Aloe vera is used to heal burns."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Teacher prompt: "Stomachache, ginger" -> Student 1: "For a stomachache, you should use ginger." Student 2: "Ginger is used to treat stomachaches."</li>
                        </ul>
                        Teacher monitors, provides feedback on accuracy and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Pair Interaction: Question and Answer:</b> Students receive a list of plants and common ailments. In pairs, they ask and answer questions about uses and advice.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What is aloe vera used for?" Student B: "Aloe vera is used to treat burns. You should apply it to the skin."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What should I use for infections?" Student B: "You should use guanabana. It's a useful herb."</li>
                        </ul>
                        They peer-check each other's responses for correct grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency and interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Family Remedy Exchange" Role-Play</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. Each student takes on the role of a family member (e.g., Grandparent, Parent, Child) sharing their knowledge of Panamanian home remedies. They must discuss a common health problem (e.g., a cold, stomachache, minor cut, stress) and offer advice using different medicinal plants, ensuring they use the target grammar ("used to", "should/shouldn't") and vocabulary. Each student must contribute at least two pieces of advice or information about a plant's uses.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student 1 (e.g., Grandparent):</b> "Oh, my back is hurting! What should I do?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student 2 (e.g., Parent):</b> "You should apply sábila (aloe vera) gel. It is traditionally used to soothe muscle pain."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student 3 (e.g., Child):</b> "Yes, and my friend's grandma says lemongrass tea is also useful for relaxing."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The conversation should flow naturally, with students exchanging ideas and asking follow-up questions about the plant benefits and applications.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher monitors groups, encourages fluency, and notes examples of correct and incorrect usage for later feedback. The focus is on getting the message across and interacting effectively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of speaking skills and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual student contributions during the "Family Remedy Exchange" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student's participation in the group discussion, demonstrating their ability to present information and exchange ideas about medicinal plants.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, including compound nouns and correct syllable stress (e.g., 'guanabana', 'treatment').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately using at least 4-5 key vocabulary words (e.g., soursop, infections, treat, soothe, apply, remedy, medicinal).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Fluency:</b> Speaking without excessive hesitation, expressing ideas clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Correctly using "used to + infinitive" for purpose and "should/shouldn't" for advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively listening and responding to group members.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts orally or in writing:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I can say about medicinal plants is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to (give advice / ask questions about plants) because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Connecting the current lesson to future learning and planning for instructional improvements.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's speaking activities did you enjoy the most? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one question you still have about using medicinal plants, or one thing you want to practice more?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What speaking activities worked best to engage students and promote the use of target language? (e.g., substitution drills, role-play)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which grammatical structures or vocabulary words were most challenging for students to use accurately in speech? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., sentence frames, more visuals) are needed for future speaking practice or the next lesson?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I better integrate pronunciation practice (e.g., compound nouns, syllable division) into speaking tasks?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary and grammar practiced today to write short descriptions or advice about medicinal plants, applying the structures "used to" and "should/shouldn't" in written form. They will construct sentences and paragraphs based on the information shared orally in this lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>